Weezy joined Wake Up Barstool on Tuesday (May 26), during which he hinted that Tha Carter VII is &#8220;coming soon,&#8221; but pumped the brakes on confirming that would be his next album, as he&#8217;s got &#8220;music for days&#8221; on deck.

&#8220; Tha Carter VII is coming soon,&#8221; he said. &#8220;I’m not sure if we going to just name my next album Tha Carter VII , but I got albums coming as well.&#8221;

Wayne then described the creative process, which includes an unidentified person or entity grabbing a bunch of songs and dictating the title, tracklist and direction of his next album. It&#8217;s vague, and unclear who he&#8217;s referring to, if he has a management team that helps him through the process or it&#8217;s his label&#8217;s choice.

&#8220;But I don’t know when, if they going to just name it Tha Carter VII or they&#8217;re going to wait for another album and name it that, you know. But I got music for days,&#8221; he continued. &#8220;What they do is they come in here and grab a bunch of songs and put a title, &#8216;This Carter VII . Carter VII coming soon.&#8217; I make so many types of music and so many genres. Whenever we have different subjects as long as I have a song about everything.&#8221;

Tha Carter VI arrived last June and debuted at No. 2 on the Billboard 200 with 108,000 total album units. However, the project was panned by critics. Wayne&#8217;s yet to release a single in 2026.

Elsewhere in the interview, Wayne also revealed his reaction to Drake getting a portrait of Weezy&#8217;s face tattooed on his arm , which came as a surprise, but Weezy was mad he has no room left to return the favor. &#8220;He&#8217;s a surprising type of guy. He really love surprises,&#8221; he said of Drake. &#8220;He loves surprising people. So yes, he definitely surprised me. It was amazing, I was mad I didn&#8217;t have no space to get him.&#8221;

Lil Wayne&#8217;s gearing up for a busy summer, as he&#8217;ll be hitting the road for a North American tour celebrating more than 20 years of Tha Carter series. He&#8217;s also set to launch a podcast with WFAN radio personality Craig Carton, The Joint Venture , which will debut with a live episode at Fanatics Fest in NYC on July 19.

